.TH PPAKZ 1 "" "ppakz" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ppakz \- pack a directory tree into a compressed Promptuarium container, and unpack it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ppakz pack
.I DIRECTORY FILE
.br
.B ppakz unpack
.I FILE DIRECTORY
.RB [ \-\-strict ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-size=\fIBYTES\fR ]
.br
.B ppakz list
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-max\-size=\fIBYTES\fR ]
.br
.B ppakz verify
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-max\-size=\fIBYTES\fR ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ppakz
is
.BR ppak (1)
in a binary form, signed
.BR PRMT:PPAKZ1\-LE :
the flags of the info nodes and the kinds of the entries are 16\-bit numbers from 0x100, and the content of every
file is compressed (a gzip stream; the algorithm is stored next to the content).
.PP
As in
.BR ppak ,
only the names and the contents are mandatory. An error in them (a wrong signature, an invalid or repeated name, an
entry with both a content and entries, or neither, an unknown compression algorithm, a content that does not
decompress) stops
.BR ppakz ,
before anything is written. A discrepancy in the additional information (a size or a CRC\-32 that does not match the
decompressed content, a type that contradicts it, a value that is not valid) is a warning, written to the standard
error with the path of the entry, and by default the content is used as it is.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B pack \fIDIRECTORY FILE\fR
Packs the contents of the directory (not the directory itself) into the file.
.TP
.B unpack \fIFILE DIRECTORY\fR [\fB\-\-strict\fR]
Checks the whole archive (every content is decompressed once), then unpacks it into the directory. With
.BR \-\-strict ,
a warning in the archive is an error, and nothing is written.
.TP
.B list \fIFILE\fR
Lists the entries, with the size and the compressed size.
.TP
.B verify \fIFILE\fR
Checks the archive without unpacking it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-max\-size=\fIBYTES\fR
The limit of the decompressed contents of the archive, together (4 GiB by default). A longer archive is an error, so
a small file can not unpack into a huge one.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success;
.B verify
only if there is no warning.
.TP
.B 1
An error, warnings with
.BR "verify" ,
or warnings with
.BR "unpack \-\-strict" .
.TP
.B 2
Wrong usage.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ppak (1),
.BR pedit (1)
